<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    xunit.runner.json caps test parallelism at 4 threads. It has to be copied next to the
    assembly or the runner never reads it, and a silently-ignored config is worse than none
    because it looks applied.

    Why cap at all: xunit defaults maxParallelThreads to the core count (12 here), and this
    repo is worked by several agents in parallel git worktrees, so the real figure is
    12 x N-agents. On 2026-07-31, with three checkouts building at once, this 12-core /
    16GB machine sat at load 364 with 18.49% CPU idle and 32M of 16G unused — blocked on
    page faults, not computing. RAM is the scarce resource, and each thread here spins up a
    WebApplicationFactory with its own SQLite database.

    That is also a correctness argument, not just a speed one: at that load the presign TTL
    assertion in StorageWiringTests.Download_url_honours_a_custom_ttl failed three runs
    straight (X-Amz-Expires 594, 596, 597 against an expected 599-600) because the two clock
    reads it straddles drifted seconds apart. Tests that were never touched should not go red.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="xunit.runner.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Store.Catalog\Store.Catalog.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Store.Api\Store.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
